scored 223 to 188. The defense has done most of the work, ranking 15th in EPA allowed while the offense sits 25th in EPA per play.","offense":"The Falcons offense has been well below average by EPA per play so far, and it has been much better running the ball than passing it. They have been one of the most run-heavy teams in the league, passing less often than game situations would predict. Michael Penix Jr. has run the passing game and leaned heavily on Drake London, who has drawn 28% of the targets.","defense":"The Falcons defense has been about average by EPA allowed per play so far. Even its strongest area, EPA allowed per play, only ranks 15th, and it is 25th in success rate allowed. Za'Darius Smith has led the way with 4.5 sacks. Their scheme has been fairly conventional, and the closest thing to a signature is an eight man box rate that ranks 8th lowest in the league.","special":"The Falcons have been about average in how often they go for it on fourth down. Their kickers have made 72% of field goals, which is one of the worst in the league. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the offensive line, about 29% of the money on the books. Their offensive line was among the oldest in the league.","players":"Michael Penix Jr. has played nearly every snap at quarterback and thrown for 1,444 yards. Drake London has been the clear top target with 28% of the looks and 692 receiving yards. Bijan Robinson has handled most of the work in the backfield with 72% of the carries. On defense, Za'Darius Smith leads the team with 4.5 sacks.","coaching":"Kevin Stefanski is calling the offense as head coach and Jeff Ulbrich is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2022 Panthers and the 2022 Titans. Their defense looks most like the 2023 49ers and the 2025 Jaguars under Anthony Campanile.","trends":"The offense went from 24th in EPA per play in 2025 to 25th so far this season, about where it was. The defense moved from 15th in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 15th so far this season, about where it was. The biggest change in approach was shotgun rate, which went from 81% to 53%.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}